# Git Flow

When creating branches, opening PRs, merging, tagging, or planning a release.

## Source of truth

Follow classic Git Flow: `https://nvie.com/posts/a-successful-git-branching-model/` (Atlassian Gitflow-compatible).

## Long-lived branches

- `main` — production
- `develop` — integration

If `develop` is missing, create it from `main` before the first feature/release branch (or ask the user if unclear).

## Short-lived branches

- `feature/<name>` — branch from `develop`, merge back into `develop`
- `release/<version>` — branch from `develop`, merge into `main` and `develop`, tag on `main`
- `hotfix/<name>` — branch from `main`, merge into `main` and `develop`, tag on `main`

Use laconic kebab-case names (`feature/home-hero`, `hotfix/api-timeout`).

## PR targets

- `feature/*` → `develop`
- `release/*` → `main` (and merge back to `develop`)
- `hotfix/*` → `main` (and merge back to `develop`)

## Default feature workflow (agreed)

Use this loop for ordinary work unless the user asks otherwise:

1. Start from up-to-date `develop` (`git checkout develop && git pull`).
2. Create `feature/<name>` from `develop`.
3. Commit only on the feature branch (never directly on `main` / `develop`).
4. Push the feature branch and open a PR into `develop`.
5. After the user asks to merge: merge the PR into `develop`, delete the feature branch, checkout `develop`, pull.
6. Continue the next task from step 1.

Do not skip the feature branch “for small changes” (rules, config, docs count too).

## Preferences

- Do not commit feature work directly onto `main` or `develop`.
- Do not push to `main` unless the user explicitly asks.
- This rule guides Git Flow when the user requests branch / PR / merge / release work — it does not start git operations on its own.
- Commit and push only when the user asks; still respect the project's git safety rules.
- Merge PRs and return to `develop` only when the user asks.
